  "articlePanels": [
    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>A report is only as good as your notes. Vague words, missing photos and actions with no owner are the usual faults. They all come from writing it up from memory at the van.</p><p>This guide shows how to write a site inspection report that holds up. It covers what to include, where reports go wrong, and what clear notes look like.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>Every report needs a header, scope, method, findings with proof, actions with owners and dates, and sign-off.</li><li>Three things ruin reports: vague words, no photos, and actions with no owner or date.</li><li>Write it on site. Notes made in the moment are clearer.</li><li>An action with no owner and no date is only a note.</li><li>Need a layout? See an <a href=\"/pages/inspection-report-template-uk.html\">inspection report template</a>.</li><li>A voice note spoken at the Fault beats a note typed an hour later.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Structure",
      "heading": "The six sections",
      "html": "<p>The layout hardly changes from job to job. The content does. Keep all six sections.</p><p><strong>Header.</strong> Site name and address, client, date and time, your name and role, a report reference. Someone must be able to read it in two years and see who wrote it, when, and for which site.</p><p><strong>Scope.</strong> What you covered and what you did not. This is the section people forget. If you could not reach the flat roof over the plant room, say so. A report with no scope claims you saw everything.</p><p><strong>Method.</strong> How you did it. Looking only, or testing too? Did you read drawings or old reports?</p><p><strong>Findings, actions and sign-off.</strong> See below.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The core",
      "heading": "Findings, actions and sign-off",
      "html": "<p><strong>Findings.</strong> Each one gives the place, what you saw, whether it meets the standard, and a photo if you can see it. This is the heart of the report.</p><p><strong>Actions.</strong> What to do, who does it and by when. Put them in a numbered list. Give each a priority. Without an owner and a date, an action is only advice.</p><p><strong>Sign-off.</strong> Your signature and the date. Some inspections also need your qualification or a statement. For a layout, see our <a href=\"/pages/inspection-report-template-uk.html\">inspection report template</a>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Common mistakes",
      "heading": "Where reports fall short",
      "html": "<p><strong>Vague words.</strong> The most common fault. Compare these two.</p><p><strong>Weak:</strong> \"Brickwork to north elevation in deteriorating condition.\"</p><p><strong>Strong:</strong> \"Spalling brickwork, north elevation, first floor. About 3m run. Mortar missing. Two areas of bare brick face. One brick face has come off. Water probably getting in. Photo 7. Specialist repair within 30 days.\"</p><p>The strong one can be acted on. Good notes give the place, the size, the proof and a date.</p><p><strong>No photo.</strong> Words about a defect are weaker than a photo. A photo cannot be argued with.</p><p><strong>No owner or date.</strong> An action list with neither is a wish list.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Voice notes",
      "heading": "Record it on the spot",
      "html": "<p>When you stand at a Fault, you say what you see. It is quick and honest. An hour later you only say what you remember.</p><p>With Quickler you send voice notes into WhatsApp as you walk. They are written out for you. The report holds your words from the moment. See <a href=\"/pages/voice-notes-site-reports-uk.html\">voice notes for site reports</a>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Actions",
      "heading": "Actions: the part people skip",
      "html": "<p>Findings say what you found. Actions say what to do about it. Most reports fall short here.</p><p>Each action needs three things. What to do. Who does it. By when. Twenty Faults with no actions is a list of problems, not a plan.</p><p>Add a priority. Urgent, required or monitor. Otherwise the client has to guess. For a worked example, see <a href=\"/pages/construction-site-inspection-report-uk.html\">construction site inspection report</a>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "faq",
      "heading": "Questions, answered",
      "html": "<h3>What must a site inspection report include?</h3><p>The site, the date and time, who inspected, what you covered and what you did not, how you did it, your findings with photos, actions with an owner and a date, and your sign-off.</p><h3>What is the difference between a finding and an observation?</h3><p>An observation is what you saw. A finding is an observation judged against a standard, as compliant or not. A good report keeps the two apart.</p><h3>How specific should observations be?</h3><p>Specific enough that someone who was not there knows what and where. \"Deteriorating condition\" is too vague. \"About 3m of spalling brickwork, north side, first floor, mortar missing. Photo 7\" is right.</p><h3>Can I use voice notes for observations?</h3><p>Yes. You speak what you see as you stand in front of it. Quickler writes the words out in the WhatsApp chat, so you do not type.</p>"
    }
